Compose Concurrent Vector Stroke Tile Renderer
Context
A Jetpack Compose drawing surface renders an infinite vector scene made of user strokes. Strokes can be created, appended point-by-point, ended, removed, restyled, undone/redone, and cleared.
Rendering is tile-based. Each 256 x 256 screen-space tile is rasterized on a background thread, then committed later for drawing. The UI must never block waiting for raster jobs.
The hard part is concurrency correctness: raster jobs may finish out of order, strokes may mutate while jobs are running, viewport changes invalidate all tile work, undo/redo can restore or remove strokes, stale raster results must be rejected, and unaffected pending/committed tiles must survive dirty-only stroke edits.
Task
Implement ConcurrentStrokeCanvas and StrokeCanvasState.

